<v Mathilda>是啊。

00:44:22.800 --> 00:44:31.470
<v Molly Ringwald>OK, well, you know what? That's really good for me to know. I had no idea. Like, when did I make you feel like that?
<v Molly Ringwald>好的，你知道吗？这对我来说真的很好。我完全不知道。比如，我什么时候让你有那种感觉？

00:44:31.470 --> 00:44:35.500
<v Mathilda>When you kept on saying, like, I wish I did better in school.
<v Mathilda>当你一直说，比如，我希望我在学校表现得更好时。

00:44:35.500 --> 00:44:38.650
<v Molly Ringwald>Oh, because I said that I wish I did better in school? So--
<v Molly Ringwald>噢，因为我说我希望我在学校表现得更好？所以……

00:44:38.650 --> 00:44:44.560
<v Mathilda>And like, you wanted me to do good.
<v Mathilda>而且你希望我表现好。

00:44:44.560 --> 00:44:47.470
<v Molly Ringwald>Oh, I'm sorry I made you feel that way.
<v Molly Ringwald>噢，对不起我让你有那种感觉。

00:44:47.470 --> 00:44:50.460
<v Mathilda>But you don't anymore.
<v Mathilda>但你现在不这样了。

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:56.410
<v Ira Glass>Do you remember the things she's talking about, of you saying to her, like, oh, I wish that I had done better in school?
<v Ira Glass>你记得她谈论的那件事吗，关于你说，噢，我希望我在学校表现更好？

00:44:56.410 --> 00:45:14.790
<v Molly Ringwald>[SIGHS] I was really surprised. I was not expecting that at all. And the only thing that I can think of, really, is we have this homework battle. And it's incredibly frustrating. And--
<v Molly Ringwald>[叹气] 我真的很惊讶。我完全没预料到。我唯一能想到的就是我们之间那场作业大战。这简直让人抓狂。而且……

00:45:14.790 --> 00:45:16.630
<v Ira Glass>It's frustrating to get her to do the work.
<v Ira Glass>让她做作业真是令人沮丧。

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:23.730
<v Molly Ringwald>Yeah because it's really easy. I mean and I'm not just saying that as an adult. I mean, it's easy.
<v Molly Ringwald>是啊，因为这很容易。我是说，我不是仅仅作为成年人这么说。我是说，这很容易。

00:45:23.730 --> 00:45:24.820
<v Ira Glass>It's easy work for her.
<v Ira Glass>这对她来说是简单的任务。

00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:43.110
<v Molly Ringwald>It's easy work for her. If she would just sit down, do it, it would take 15 minutes, 20 minutes. But she resents the fact that she has to do it so much, and it became such a battle, that she would sort of lie, sprawled out, kind of barely write-- you couldn't even read her writing.
<v Molly Ringwald>这对她来说很简单。如果她能坐下来做，只需要15分钟，20分钟。但她非常厌恶不得不去做这件事，这成了一场大战，她会躺在那里，伸展开来，几乎不怎么写字——你甚至读不懂她的字。

00:45:43.110 --> 00:45:57.240
<v Molly Ringwald>And I would just get so frustrated with her, and you know, and I would yell at her and say, you can do better than this. You're smarter than this. You know, all the things that parents say. And I think it must have affected her.
<v Molly Ringwald>我会对她感到非常沮丧，你知道，我会对她大喊大叫说，你可以做得比这更好。你比这更聪明。你知道，所有父母都会说的话。我觉得这一定影响了她。

00:45:57.240 --> 00:46:08.980
<v Molly Ringwald>And then, she said, well, you don't do it anymore. You know, and the reason why I don't do it anymore is because I don't do her homework with her anymore, because I can't. I find it too frustrating.
<v Molly Ringwald>然后，她说，嗯，你不再那样了。你知道，我不再那样做的原因是，我不再陪她写作业了，因为我做不到。我觉得太令人沮丧了。

00:46:08.980 --> 00:46:12.310
<v Ira Glass>When she said that, I thought, like, oh, is she just being protective of you, you know?
<v Ira Glass>当她说那话时，我心想，噢，她是仅仅在保护你吗？你知道？

00:46:12.310 --> 00:46:40.810
<v Molly Ringwald>I think she was being protective of me, too. I think the thing that I noticed the most was Mathilda kind of wanting to make me feel OK. She really did not want to hurt my feelings or make me upset, and she wants to please me, too. I can hear that when I-- [SIGHS] Yeah.
<v Molly Ringwald>我觉得她也在保护我。我注意到最多的是Mathilda那种想要让我感觉良好的愿望。她真的不想伤害我的感情或者让我难过，而且她也想讨好我。当我……[叹气] 是的，我能听到那个。

00:46:40.810 --> 00:46:47.420
<v Ira Glass>Well, the fact that the next thing that happens-- she instantly goes to, I have better parents than they do.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，接下来发生的事实是——她立刻转向说，我有比他们更好的父母。

00:46:47.420 --> 00:46:50.850
<v Molly Ringwald>I know, like it's scripted. I know.
<v Molly Ringwald>我知道，就像写好的剧本。我知道。

00:46:50.850 --> 00:46:55.230
<v Molly Ringwald>So is there anything else that you got out of the movie that you--
<v Molly Ringwald>所以你从电影里还得到了什么……

00:46:55.230 --> 00:47:00.210
<v Mathilda>Um, like, well, I have better parents than them.
<v Mathilda>嗯，就像，好吧，我有比他们更好的父母。

00:47:00.210 --> 00:47:05.870
<v Molly Ringwald>[LAUGHS] You're just saying that to make me feel better. Come on.
<v Molly Ringwald>[笑] 你只是为了让我感觉更好才这么说。来吧。

00:47:05.870 --> 00:47:22.400
<v Molly Ringwald>I mean, it was bizarre how she just said the thing that I hoped she would get out of it. She knows. I mean, she can intuit that. She knew that I was hoping for that. Yeah, she was giving me a little present, wrapped in a bow.
<v Molly Ringwald>我是说，她竟然脱口而出我希望她从中得到的东西，这太奇怪了。她知道。我是说，她能直觉到。她知道我在希望那样。是的，她给了我一份用蝴蝶结包装的小礼物。

00:47:22.400 --> 00:47:24.780
<v Ira Glass>But at a moment where it doesn't feel like that at all.
<v Ira Glass>但在那一刻，感觉根本不是那样。

00:47:24.780 --> 00:47:27.860
<v Molly Ringwald>No. No, not at all.
<v Molly Ringwald>不。不，完全不是。

00:47:27.860 --> 00:47:51.830
<v Ira Glass>The whole thing, after wondering a lot about what happened when Mathilda cried and how she handled it, and should she have let Mathilda talk for longer, and should she have asked her more questions or different questions-- you know, you just can never know what things that you say to your kids are going to stay with them, and just little things, said in a passing moment, that are going to bounce around in their heads and lead them to conclusions that you don't intend or expect in any way.
<v Ira Glass>整件事，在想了很久关于Mathilda哭泣时发生了什么、她是怎么处理的、Molly是否应该让Mathilda说久一点、是否应该问她更多或不同的问题——你知道，你永远无法知道你对孩子说的哪些话会留在他们心中，那些在转瞬即逝的时刻里说出的小事，会在他们脑海里跳动，导致他们得出你根本没预料到的结论。

00:47:51.830 --> 00:48:20.610
<v Molly Ringwald>Yeah. I think there's always moments where you perceive things differently. I know it with my own mom and dad. I mean, there are times where I'll tell a story that I've heard a million times over the years, you know, and my mom will just completely switch it up, or she'll see it completely differently.
<v Molly Ringwald>是啊。我觉得总有那么一些时刻，你对事情的感知不同。我对我自己的妈妈和爸爸也是这样。我是说，有的时候我会讲一个我多年来听过几百万遍的故事，你知道，我妈妈会完全改写它，或者她会看到完全不同的东西。

00:48:20.610 --> 00:48:24.650
<v Ira Glass>Like some story from your childhood, like you're telling this story about something they did or--
<v Ira Glass>比如你童年的某个故事，就像你正在讲述某件他们做过的事或者……

00:48:24.650 --> 00:48:25.170
<v Molly Ringwald>Yeah.
<v Molly Ringwald>是啊。

00:48:25.170 --> 00:48:26.760
<v Ira Glass>And they're just like, no, no, no.
<v Ira Glass>然后他们只是说，不，不，不。

00:48:26.760 --> 00:48:39.930
<v Molly Ringwald>Well, yeah. I mean, there's one. You know, I come from a family where my sister was sort of designated as the great beauty in the family. And this was just, like, known. My sister--
<v Molly Ringwald>好吧，是啊。我是说，有一个。你知道，我来自一个家庭，我的姐姐被视为家庭里的绝世美女。这简直是……公认的。我的姐姐……

00:48:39.930 --> 00:48:58.580
<v Molly Ringwald>I was the talented one, my brother was the smart one, and my sister was the beautiful one. And I remember actually asking my mom at-- I must have been around Mathilda's age-- if she thought that I was pretty. And she said, you're cute. And--
<v Molly Ringwald>我是有才华的那个，我哥哥是聪明的那个，我姐姐是漂亮的那个。我记得我确实问过我妈妈——那时候我大概和Mathilda年纪差不多大——她是否觉得我漂亮。她说，你很可爱。而且……

00:48:58.580 --> 00:48:59.060
<v Ira Glass>Ooh.
<v Ira Glass>噢。

00:48:59.060 --> 00:49:14.750
<v Molly Ringwald>Yeah, and that is really not what you want to hear when you're-- (LAUGHING) 10 years-- I mean, now, it's OK. I would be OK with cute. But when you're 10, it was just devastating.
<v Molly Ringwald>是啊，当你10岁的时候，那真不是你想听到的。（笑）我是说，现在，没关系。我会接受“可爱”。但当你10岁的时候，那简直是毁灭性的。

00:49:14.750 --> 00:49:28.140
<v Molly Ringwald>And-- and she completely denies that now. And I mean, something that would have such an impact on me-- I mean, I just-- I wasn't making it up. It really affected me.
<v Molly Ringwald>而且……她现在完全否认这一点。我是说，这种对我产生那么大影响的事情——我……我并不是编造的。它真的影响了我。

00:49:28.140 --> 00:49:30.060
<v Ira Glass>And she just says it didn't happen.
<v Ira Glass>她只是说那没发生。

00:49:30.060 --> 00:49:34.550
<v Molly Ringwald>She says, oh, I always knew that you were beautiful. You know, ask your father.
<v Molly Ringwald>她说，噢，我一直都知道你很漂亮。你知道，去问你父亲。

00:49:34.550 --> 00:49:42.690
<v Ira Glass>And obviously, like, if she had any idea how it would bounce around in your head, she would have never said it, too. She didn't think like, oh, that's going to stick.
<v Ira Glass>显然，如果她知道这会在你脑子里怎么跳动，她也绝不会那样说。她没想过，噢，那会让人耿耿于怀。

00:49:42.690 --> 00:49:46.310
<v Molly Ringwald>(LAUGHING) No, no. I don't think that she did.
<v Molly Ringwald>[笑] 不，不。我不觉得她有那种想法。

00:49:46.310 --> 00:49:53.300
<v Ira Glass>I have a friend. Her mom would tell her and her sister, no, you girls are average.
<v Ira Glass>我有位朋友。她妈妈总是对她和她妹妹说，不，你们女孩很普通。

00:49:53.300 --> 00:49:54.710
<v Molly Ringwald>Oh!
<v Molly Ringwald>噢！

00:49:54.710 --> 00:50:05.760
<v Ira Glass>No, no, like, you guys are-- you girls are average. You know, like, you're smart, but you're average smart. And I was like, wow, you were not raised by Jews, man. [LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>不，不，就像，你们女孩很普通。你知道，就像，你们很聪明，但你们的聪明很普通。我心想，哇，你没在犹太人家里长大，哥们。[笑声]

00:50:05.760 --> 00:50:16.380
<v Ira Glass>That is not the message you get. I mean, in my experience, there's a lot of, you're so special. You're the most special. You're so special. You know, like, the boys and the girls. You know, like, she's the most talented--
<v Ira Glass>那不是你得到的讯息。我是说，在我的经验里，有很多“你是如此特别”。“你是最特别的”。“你是如此特别”。你知道，就像，男孩和女孩。你知道，就像，她是最有才华的……

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:27.120
<v Molly Ringwald>Well, I was always told that I was special. I mean, there was no question that I was special and that I was destined for greatness.
<v Molly Ringwald>嗯，我总是被告知我很特别。我是说，我毫无疑问很特别，而且命中注定会成就伟大。

00:50:27.120 --> 00:50:27.930
<v Ira Glass>As a little kid?
<v Ira Glass>小时候？

00:50:27.930 --> 00:50:28.740
<v Molly Ringwald>As a little kid.
<v Molly Ringwald>小时候。

00:50:28.740 --> 00:50:28.980
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:50:28.980 --> 00:51:09.930
<v Molly Ringwald>From the time that I was really little. I mean, to the point where-- [CHUCKLES] this is kind of heavy, but I'll tell you anyway. My first brother died. He was the first, and I was the last, so we never met. But my mom was understandably just devastated by this and was sort of suicidal for a while. I mean, didn't actually try anything, but she was considering, and then was-- [LAUGHS] this makes her sound so hippy dippy, and she's not at all.
<v Molly Ringwald>从我还是很小的时候起。我是说，到那种程度——[轻笑] 这有点沉重，但我还是告诉你吧。我的第一个哥哥去世了。他是第一个，我是最后一个，所以我们从没见过面。但我妈妈显然为此心碎，有一段时间甚至有点自杀倾向。我是说，没真的尝试什么，但她在考虑，然后……[笑] 这让她听起来很嬉皮，但她一点也不。

00:51:09.930 --> 00:51:34.450
<v Molly Ringwald>But she believes that she conversed with a spirit. And what they said, basically-- and this is a story that I've heard since I was very small-- that she was here for another reason, for someone else. And as soon as I was born, she knew that it was me.
<v Molly Ringwald>但她相信她和灵魂对话过。他们说的话，基本上——这是我从小听到的故事——她来这里是为了另一个原因，为了另一个人。我一出生，她就知道是我。

00:51:34.450 --> 00:51:36.130
<v Ira Glass>That's a lot to put on you.
<v Ira Glass>那对你来说压力太大了。

00:51:36.130 --> 00:51:39.400
<v Molly Ringwald>Yeah. It's heavy. (LAUGHING) It's really--
<v Molly Ringwald>是啊。这很沉重。（笑）这真的很……

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:44.530
<v Ira Glass>She told you that when you were a little girl, that she was put on this Earth because of you?
<v Ira Glass>她告诉你那话时你还是个小女孩，说她来到地球是因为你？

00:51:44.530 --> 00:51:56.660
<v Molly Ringwald>She believed that that was why-- yeah, I mean, because she knew that there was some reason why she was supposed to stick around and--
<v Molly Ringwald>她相信那确实是原因——是啊，我是说，因为她知道她命中注定要留下来的某个原因，为了……

00:51:56.660 --> 00:52:02.830
<v Ira Glass>And stay alive. And it was to have this little girl, who was you, who was such a special gift to the world.
<v Ira Glass>为了活下去。而那是为了拥有这个小女孩，也就是你，你是给世界的一份特别礼物。

00:52:02.830 --> 00:52:04.180
<v Molly Ringwald>Yeah.
<v Molly Ringwald>是啊。

00:52:04.180 --> 00:52:08.230
<v Ira Glass>So how strange that you would end up famous by the age of 15 or something.
<v Ira Glass>所以你竟然在15岁左右就出名了，这太奇怪了。

00:52:08.230 --> 00:52:12.460
<v Molly Ringwald>Well, I kind of had to. I mean, I kept my mom alive.
<v Molly Ringwald>嗯，我不得不出名。我是说，我让我妈活了下来。

00:52:12.460 --> 00:52:21.880
<v Ira Glass>And so then, when you actually did become a movie star as a teenager, did she take that as proof? Like, oh, see, that was all true?
<v Ira Glass>所以当你十几岁时真的成了电影明星，她把它当作证明吗？比如，噢，你看，那全是真的？

00:52:21.880 --> 00:52:22.420
<v Molly Ringwald>Yeah.
<v Molly Ringwald>是啊。

00:52:22.420 --> 00:52:26.530
<v Ira Glass>And did you, at the time? Like, did the whole story fit together for you, too?
<v Ira Glass>而当时你呢？就像，整个故事对你来说也契合了吗？

00:52:26.530 --> 00:52:35.560
<v Molly Ringwald>Yeah. I had to succeed. I had to be great.
<v Molly Ringwald>是啊。我必须成功。我必须成就伟大。

00:52:35.560 --> 00:52:38.380
<v Ira Glass>What a lot of pressure on you.
<v Ira Glass>你压力太大了。

00:52:38.380 --> 00:52:45.850
<v Molly Ringwald>I know. And then, what do I do? I turn around, and I pressure my daughter, because I think she's so great. I know.
<v Molly Ringwald>我知道。然后呢，我做了什么？我转过头来，给我女儿施压，因为我觉得她太棒了。我知道。

00:52:45.850 --> 00:52:47.890
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:52:47.890 --> 00:53:29.610
<v Nancy Updike>Ira J. Glass with Molly Ringwald. She's still acting and translating, and she's working on a new book about her Paris years. And Mathilda-- she's in college now and just finished filming her first movie.
<v Nancy Updike>Ira J. Glass与Molly Ringwald。她依然在演戏和翻译，正在写一本关于她巴黎岁月的书。而Mathilda——她现在在读大学，刚拍完她的第一部电影。

00:53:29.610 --> 00:53:45.840
<v Nancy Updike>Our program was produced today by Jonathan Menjivar, with Alex Blumberg, Ben Calhoun, Sean Cole, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Sarah Koenig, Brian Reed, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Ira Glass. Our senior producer for this show was Julie Snyder.
<v Nancy Updike>我们节目今天由Jonathan Menjivar制作，Alex Blumberg、Ben Calhoun、Sean Cole、Stephanie Foo、Chana Joffe-Walt、Sarah Koenig、Brian Reed、Robyn Semien、Alissa Shipp和Ira Glass协助。我们节目的高级制作人是Julie Snyder。

00:53:45.840 --> 00:54:05.640
<v Nancy Updike>Production help from Simon Adler and Lilly Sullivan, additional production help on today's rerun from James Bennett II, Michael Comite Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, and Matt Tierney. Our website-- thisamericanlife.org, where you can stream over 800 of our episodes for absolutely free.
<v Nancy Updike>Simon Adler和Lilly Sullivan提供了制作帮助，James Bennett II、Michael Comite、Katherine Rae Mondo、Stowe Nelson和Matt Tierney在今天重播节目中提供了额外制作帮助。我们的网站——thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以免费收听我们超过800期的节目。

00:54:05.640 --> 00:54:18.640
<v Nancy Updike>Thanks, as always, to our program's confounder, Torey Malatia, and to our boss, Mr. Ira Glass. While he was hosting the show recently, he spent a little time moonlighting as a realtor. He's really got to work on his pitch.
<v Nancy Updike>一如既往地感谢我们节目的创始人Torey Malatia，以及我们的老板Ira Glass先生。当他最近主持节目时，他花了一些时间兼职做房地产经纪人。他真的得好好练练他的推销话术。

00:54:18.640 --> 00:54:21.240
<v Domingo Martinez>Look at that really [BLEEP] small house over there.
<v Domingo Martinez>看看那边那个真的[消音]小的房子。

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:26.240
<v Nancy Updike>I'm Nancy Updike. Ira Glass will be back with more stories of This American Life.
<v Nancy Updike>我是Nancy Updike。Ira Glass将带着更多《美国生活》的故事回归。
